WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.109 --> 00:00:02.040
- [L.A Buckner] Cootie Williams,

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.497
Duke Ellington's trumpet soloist,

00:00:03.497 --> 00:00:05.127
called Charlie Bird Parker,

00:00:05.127 --> 00:00:07.750
"the greatest individual
musician that ever lived,"

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:10.340
and in the history of jazz
there had never been one man

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.570
who influenced all instruments.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.410
So what exactly did Bird
do to have such influence,

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:18.640
and how did he do it all before
dying at only 34 years old?

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.130
In this episode of Sound Field,

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:21.840
we'll take a brief look at how Parker's

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.550
relentless practicing helped inspire

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.120
an evolution in bebop with another

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.037
Kansas city jazz musician.

00:00:27.037 --> 00:00:29.870
(saxophone music)

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.300
There are many sub-genres of jazz,

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.080
including Parker's bebop,

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.150
but there's one really
easy way to differentiate

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:38.860
bebop from all other types.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:41.840
All you gotta do is try to
dance to it when you hear it.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.018
Go ahead and try.

00:00:43.018 --> 00:00:45.685
(Chicago plays)

00:00:47.486 --> 00:00:49.986
(Bebop plays)

00:00:52.515 --> 00:00:55.765
(Cheek to cheek plays)

00:00:57.901 --> 00:01:01.848
(Fine and dandy plays)

00:01:01.848 --> 00:01:02.880
Here's the thing,

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.310
Charlie Parker didn't care
if you danced to his music.

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:08.700
He said himself he's not an
entertainer, but an artist.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.650
His desire to be an
artist, and to improvise,

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.710
began at an early age in his
home town of Kansas City.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.740
To understand Charlie Parker's influence,

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:18.120
you first have to understand
how bebop came about,

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.470
and how it differs from other jazz styles.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:22.820
Now, jazz is intricate to say the least,

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.320
and historically, there's
a lot of moving parts.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.770
So here's a brief summary.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.860
Musically, jazz owes its
roots to three genres.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:31.570
The rhythm of ragtime,

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:33.990
the orchestral instruments
of concert music,

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.380
most notably from Sicilian traditions,

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.020
and the feeling of the blues.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:41.210
Geographically, there
are four cradles of jazz.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.717
New York, Chicago, Kansas City,

00:01:43.717 --> 00:01:46.600
and the birthplace of
the genre, New Orleans.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.870
Born during the start of the 20th century,

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.600
jazz grew out of
African-American communities

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.050
and bands often consisted
of the cornet or trumpet,

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:58.540
tuba, banjo and trombone.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.848
Chicago replaced the tuba and banjo

00:02:00.848 --> 00:02:02.900
with upright bass and guitar,

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.600
and added a drum kit.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.200
Greater solo space, fixed ensembles

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.300
and a more prominent role
for the rhythm section

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:10.330
are key.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.097
You can really hear
that in Benny Goodman's

00:02:12.097 --> 00:02:13.518
"Sing, Sing, Sing."

00:02:13.518 --> 00:02:16.935
(Sing, sing, Sing plays)

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.150
At the same time, Kansas City
emphasized larger ensembles,

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.860
saxophone sections, the
blues, riff melodies

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.579
and strong walking bass.

00:02:27.579 --> 00:02:30.746
(Moten's Swing plays)

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:35.130
During the late 1920's, New
York City began to experience

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:36.770
a thriving nightlife scene,

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.170
with an over-abundance
of venues available.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.370
Over the next decade,
jazz orchestras from both

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:43.890
Chicago and Kansas City packed up

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.370
and headed to Manhattan.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.320
This surge of talent allowed New York City

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.570
to adapt and develop
different types of jazz,

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:52.190
most notably, big band swing music.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.147
For example, listen to Duke Ellington's

00:02:54.147 --> 00:02:55.488
"It don't mean a thing".

00:02:55.488 --> 00:02:59.321
(It don't mean a thing plays)

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.290
During world war two, however,

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:05.430
with musicians joining the
military and heading overseas,

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.640
these large orchestras began to shrink.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.150
For this reason, the 1940's saw a surge

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:11.240
in smaller ensembles,

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:13.600
such as quartets, and quintets.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.210
Groups often consisted
of one or two horns,

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:18.298
usually saxophone and or trumpet,

00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:21.370
bass, drums and piano.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:23.780
By nature of being a smaller ensemble,

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.400
improvisation and interaction flourished.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.730
Enter Charlie Parker
and the clubs he visited

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:30.980
on 12th and Vine in Kansas City,

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:33.310
where all night jam sessions
acted as the heartbeat

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:34.930
of the local jazz scene.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:38.510
One night, at just 16 years
old, Bird tried jamming

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:40.150
but fumbled several times.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:43.610
The session ended abruptly
when Jo Jones, Basie's drummer,

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:45.640
dropped his cymbal on the floor.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.290
Young Parker was not talented enough

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:49.110
to keep up with the musical veterans.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:52.010
Humiliated, Charlie sulked back home,

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.890
and began practicing diligently,

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:56.127
vowing to return to 12th street, and quote

00:03:56.127 --> 00:03:57.780
"Show those cats."

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.160
To tell us more about the next
steps of Charlie's journey,

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.230
I reached out to Chuck Haddix,

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.500
a Charlie Parker
biographer and the director

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.740
of the Marr Sound
Archives, a collection of

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:09.150
380,000 historic sound recordings,

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.250
at the University of
Missouri, Kansas City.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.307
- Next summer, he went back to Mussers,

00:04:13.307 --> 00:04:17.083
and Mussers is in the
Ozarks area of Missouri,

00:04:17.083 --> 00:04:18.520
it's known as Little Dixie.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:20.780
It's racially hostile territory,

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:23.320
so they stayed on-site at the resort.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:25.580
Since he couldn't go out to the clubs

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:26.950
or do anything like that,

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:30.700
he took that time to practice his horn,

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:34.880
and he went to what is often
referred to as woodshedding.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.530
And he mastered his horn,
and according to all accounts

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:40.810
when he returned to Kansas
City in the fall of 1937,

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:44.620
he was musically transformed,
a transformed musician.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.180
- You said the woodshed, and historically

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:48.910
that's the place that you go where

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:50.380
nobody can hear you, and you can just

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:52.630
express yourself to the fullest, you know,

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:53.800
get all your chops out,

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.500
but that's a word that has been adopted

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.030
by the drum community.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:04:59.500
We're literally shedding
the wood on our sticks

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:01.957
and we're like, its the
mentality, they're like

00:05:01.957 --> 00:05:04.440
"We're in the woodshed", so
we gotta like give our best,

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:05.850
we gotta go there, we gotta try do things,

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:07.530
we gotta take what we
can take from each other,

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:08.990
we're gonna share what we can share.

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:10.910
It's those long hours in the shed

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:14.010
that change your playing experience,

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:16.140
that change what your able to output.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.580
One night, in 1939, after returning

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:20.390
to the jazz scene in New York,

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:22.910
Charlie Parker was playing
'Cherokee' in a jam session

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:25.320
with guitarist William 'Biddy' Fleet,

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:27.660
when he hit upon a method
for developing his solos

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.460
that enabled one of his
main musical innovations.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:34.150
He realized that the 12
semitones of the chromatic scale

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:36.280
can lead melodically to any key,

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:39.650
breaking some of the confines
of simpler jazz soloing.

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:41.920
To tell us more about
Charlie's innovation,

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.680
we talked to renowned
Kansas City jazz musician

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:46.130
Bobby Watson.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:49.860
Well I know that I'm talking
to a legend right now,

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.930
to jazz royalty.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:53.880
I know that that's true.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:55.680
- Yeah, I'm a legend in my own mind.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:58.430
- Can you talk about a little bit of

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:01.352
his discovery of the semitone changes?

00:06:01.352 --> 00:06:06.320
- All Charlie Parker did
was take Lester Young, Prez,

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.800
and he took it and he extended it.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:12.440
Prez was a very lyrical player.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:15.480
He composed on top of the changes.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:19.380
And semitone, in my world, is a half step,

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:20.963
which is going from this,

00:06:20.963 --> 00:06:23.796
(plays saxophone)

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.714
as opposed to a whole step.

00:06:27.714 --> 00:06:30.547
(plays saxophone)

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:35.534
Now, if you go a bunch of these,

00:06:35.534 --> 00:06:38.367
(plays saxophone)

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:46.980
you know, and on up, if you
do it one after the other,

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:48.592
it's a chromatic scale.

00:06:48.592 --> 00:06:52.010
(plays saxophone)

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:56.620
- The sauciest stuff, the
most killingest stuff,

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:59.660
is little chunks of chromatic movement.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:03.374
- Here's the thing, it
depends on the rhythm,

00:07:03.374 --> 00:07:05.960
of how you play the chromatic.

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:08.796
Say if I outline a dominant chord.

00:07:08.796 --> 00:07:12.280
(plays saxophone)

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:17.280
Now if I wanna outline those
chords using a chromatic scale,

00:07:17.304 --> 00:07:19.336
I would emphasize.

00:07:19.336 --> 00:07:22.369
(plays saxophone)

00:07:22.369 --> 00:07:23.310
So I go,

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:26.143
(plays saxophone)

00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:35.990
So it sounds like it fits that.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:38.550
It's the same notes, but
it's where I put the accents

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:40.328
that make it usable.

00:07:40.328 --> 00:07:42.810
- It takes the listener
on a rollercoaster ride,

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:45.270
'cause he's starting in a
place and you're not sure,

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:46.860
his accents seem like
it's gonna throw you off

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:48.760
but it always ends up making sense.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:50.920
That's so similar to, like, drummers,

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:52.460
that I end up listening to, that I like.

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:54.420
Some cats would be like,
"Oh what's happening,

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:56.860
oh, is he gonna keep the beat,
oh it stayed right on beat."

00:07:56.860 --> 00:08:00.659
Or even some, some, some rappers.

00:08:00.659 --> 00:08:02.680
- That's it, we're talking about Bird,

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:05.070
and we're talking about rhythm.

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:08.370
A strong rhythm is
better than a good note.

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:10.777
It's 'Cherokee' for your
listeners, you know,

00:08:10.777 --> 00:08:12.520
and it kinda goes like this.

00:08:14.274 --> 00:08:17.024
(plays Cherokee)

00:08:31.119 --> 00:08:32.340
Then it repeats again.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:33.173
- Right.

00:08:33.173 --> 00:08:35.110
- Prez would probably
do something like this.

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:37.602
One, two, a one two three four.

00:08:37.602 --> 00:08:40.435
(plays saxophone)

00:08:54.250 --> 00:08:57.460
And then Bird, he would take it and go...

00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:00.293
(plays saxophone)

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:13.240
He was still melodic.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:15.990
- But it was extended in depth.

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:16.823
- [Bobby] Yeah.

00:09:16.823 --> 00:09:21.150
At lightning speed he could
change directions, adapt.

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:24.110
It didn't matter where he started an idea

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:25.613
or where he ended an idea,

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:29.110
and all these things shaped my image

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:33.439
of what I think Bird was and
what qualities he possessed.

00:09:33.439 --> 00:09:36.356
(jazz music plays)

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:45.390
- Bird's technique of blistering rhythm

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:48.060
and spontaneous extended
chromatic chord structure

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:50.150
that he honed through
his time in the woodshed,

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:53.640
bought him a newfound legitimacy
in the national jazz scene.

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:55.470
This eventually lead to him headlining

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:58.180
the opening night of the New
York jazz club, Birdlane,

00:09:58.180 --> 00:09:59.850
that would become his namesake.

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:02.710
Not only did his style shape
what came to be known as bebop,

00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:04.640
but it also influenced
the styles of legends

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:06.050
that he played alongside,

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:08.710
from Dizzy Gillespie to Thelonius Monk

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:11.550
to Miles Davis to John Coltrane.

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:13.400
Elements of the resulting bebop style

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:16.455
have gone on to influence
legendary MC's like Rakim,

00:10:16.455 --> 00:10:19.788
(I ain't no joke plays)

00:10:20.735 --> 00:10:23.335
to the syncopated rhythms
of Tool drummer Danny Carey,

00:10:23.335 --> 00:10:25.918
(Pneuma plays)

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:30.175
to contemporary jazz composers
like Kamasi Washington.

00:10:30.175 --> 00:10:32.675
(Truth plays)

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:38.550
Tracking the influence of Parker's bebop

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:40.250
is like trying to follow all the ripples

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:42.170
across the face of a massive lake.

00:10:42.170 --> 00:10:43.810
The effect stretches so far,

00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:45.600
that it simply informs the subconscious

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:48.870
of nearly every aspect of
music made in the present day.

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:50.940
Bebop was about freedom of expression,

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:53.500
and escaping the harmonic
and melodic restraints

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:55.690
imposed by the old musical order.

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:57.540
So above all the technical innovations

00:10:57.540 --> 00:10:59.440
Parker brought to the world of music,

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:01.960
it's the spirit of
imagination, and emphasis

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:03.520
on imparting your own style,

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:05.500
that stands as his lasting legacy.

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:07.700
That's all we have on
Charlie Parker's bebop,

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:08.830
but if you wanna see more,

00:11:08.830 --> 00:11:10.400
check out (indistinct) video journey

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:12.040
in understanding the style from the lens

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:14.230
of a classically trained musician.

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:16.530
In the meantime, I'm
gonna be in the woodshed.

00:11:18.657 --> 00:11:22.074
(drums and jazz playing)

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:30.908
- Now if I was gonna do it,
I would do it like this.

00:11:30.908 --> 00:11:33.741
(plays saxophone)

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:49.883
That's me.

